    <body>
      <sec id="article-29352.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Regional anesthesia has evolved into a safe and effective technique, with spinal opioids playing a central role in postoperative and chronic pain management. Once considered high-risk, advances in sterile technique, drug formulation, and evidence from large-scale studies have established spinal anesthesia as a reliable option for surgical and analgesic purposes. This course reviews intrathecal opioids, eg, morphine, fentanyl, and sufentanil, which provide superior pain control by enhancing sensory block quality and reducing systemic opioid requirements. Participants will also gain an understanding of their pharmacologic differences&#x02014;particularly between hydrophilic and lipophilic agents&#x02014;to inform clinical decision-making, balancing onset, duration, and adverse effect profiles. Despite the potential for complications such as pruritus, postdural puncture headache, and dose-dependent respiratory depression, spinal opioids remain a valuable tool when appropriately selected and monitored.</p>
        <p>This activity explores spinal opioid pharmacology, indications, contraindications, and safety considerations as well as applications for intraoperative and postoperative analgesia, strategies for minimizing risks, and evidence-based approaches for integrating spinal opioids into chronic pain management.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in employing spinal opioids in anesthetic practice, performing the recommended evaluation and risk assessment, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach to enhance patient safety, improve pain outcomes, and apply individualized, mechanism-based analgesic care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the indications for using spinal opioids in anaesthetic practice.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based protocols for spinal opioid use in indicated patients.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement patient-centered monitoring plans following spinal opioid administration in anesthetic practice.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve interprofessional collaboration to optimize safety and outcomes in perioperative pain management using spinal opioids in anesthetic practice.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29352&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29352">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Regional anesthesia, consisting of spinal, caudal, and epidural blocks, was first utilized for surgical procedures at the turn of the twentieth century. Initially deemed unsafe due to reports of permanent neurologic injury, a large-scale study in the 1950s proved complications were rare when blocks were performed skillfully and with attention to sterile technique, combined with the improved safety profile of injected medications. Initial work showing improved pain management in cancer patients&#x000a0;has expanded spinal opioids for postoperative pain management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Spinal anesthesia has developed a well-established record of safety over decades of clinical use. In certain situations, clinicians regard it as the anesthetic of choice and, in select cases, the safest available option. Local anesthetics provide the foundation for surgical anesthesia, yet they are frequently paired with intrathecal opioids to enhance intraoperative analgesia and extend pain relief into the postoperative period once the local anesthetic effect diminishes.</p>
        <p>Intrathecal opioids deliver effective analgesia while avoiding sympathetic and motor blockade, offering targeted pain control with fewer physiologic disruptions. Their utility extends beyond perioperative care into the management of chronic pain. Clinicians may administer them as single injections for select patients, but more commonly employ implantable infusion pumps to deliver sustained, long-term relief in individuals with complex pain syndromes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Vertebral Anatomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>The spine consists of vertebral bones separated by intervertebral discs, providing both structure and flexibility,&#x000a0; comprising 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, and 5 lumbar vertebrae, followed by the sacrum. Posterior vertebral elements consist of the vertebral arch, spinous process, pedicles, and laminae, while the vertebral body forms the anterior portion. Thoracic spinous processes angle steeply and project inferiorly, whereas lumbar spinous processes extend in a nearly horizontal orientation. At each vertebral level, paired spinal nerves exit the central nervous system (CNS), contributing to peripheral innervation.</p>
        <p>The spinal cord occupies the vertebral column and is enclosed by the pia mater, arachnoid mater, and dura mater. Teaching emphasizes that the spinal cord extends from the foramen magnum to L1 in adults and to L3 in children, migrating cephalad with age. A lumbar puncture performed below L1 in adults or below L3 in children minimizes the risk of needle trauma. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) fills the subarachnoid or intrathecal space, accessed through successive layers: skin, subcutaneous fat, supraspinous ligament, interspinous ligament, ligamentum flavum, dura mater, subdural space, arachnoid mater, and finally the subarachnoid space.</p>
        <p>CSF circulates within the cerebral ventricles, cisterns, and subarachnoid space, cushioning the CNS and clearing metabolic waste. The choroid plexuses of the lateral ventricles produce most CSF, with smaller contributions from ventricular ependymal linings and perivascular fluid exchange. In adults, CSF production averages 21 mL/h, maintaining a total volume of approximately 150 mL. Flow proceeds from the lateral ventricles through the intraventricular foramina into the third ventricle, then via the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle, and exits through the foramen of Magendie and foramina of Luschka into the cerebellomedullary cistern. From this point, CSF enters the subarachnoid space, circulating around the brain and spinal cord before absorption through arachnoid granulations. CSF remains isotonic with plasma but contains lower concentrations of potassium, bicarbonate, and glucose, as well as limited protein. Absorption serves as the principal mechanism for returning perivascular and interstitial protein to the blood, compensating for the absence of lymphatics in the CNS.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacology</bold>
</p>
        <p>Opioids used in spinal anesthesia can be broadly categorized as hydrophilic or lipophilic. Lipophilic opioids demonstrate a rapid onset of action, diffuse quickly into surrounding tissues, and produce a shorter duration of effect with minimal rostral spread. Their primary role involves prolonging the sensory block of intrathecal local anesthetics. Hydrophilic opioids, in contrast, act more slowly but exhibit prolonged rostral spread above the injection site and slower plasma reuptake, resulting in broader distribution and extended analgesia.</p>
        <p>Five opioid receptor types have been characterized: mu (MOR), kappa (KOR), delta (DOR), nociception (NOR), and zeta (ZOR). ZOR regulates tissue growth events, while the other four contribute to analgesia. Both endogenous and exogenous opioids bind to these G-protein-coupled receptors, leading to the inhibition of voltage-gated calcium channels and the suppression of excitatory neurotransmitters, eg, substance P and glutamate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Morphine remains the most frequently used neuraxial opioid because of its long duration compared with fentanyl and sufentanil. Diamorphine, used almost exclusively in the UK, is 280 times more lipid soluble than morphine, which accounts for its shorter onset. Morphine binds primarily to mu-opioid receptor subtypes mu1 and mu2, inhibiting adenylate cyclase through G-protein signaling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r3">[3]</xref> This action increases potassium channel activity while suppressing voltage-dependent calcium channels, raising the threshold for neuronal pain transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r4">[4]</xref> Morphine stimulates opioid receptors in the substantia gelatinosa of the posterior spinal cord, and its hydrophilic nature enables slow rostral spread with prolonged analgesia, even when delivered at the lumbar level.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r5">[5]</xref> Intrathecal morphine provides approximately 18 to 24 hours of postoperative pain relief, with an onset of 45 to 75 minutes due to limited penetration across the blood-brain barrier. Adult dosing typically ranges from 0.15 to 0.3 mg.</p>
        <p>In contrast, intrathecal fentanyl (10&#x02013;20 &#x000b5;g) and sufentanil (2.5&#x02013;5 &#x000b5;g) are favored for improving intraoperative analgesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r7">[7]</xref> Their lipophilic properties account for a rapid onset, typically 5 to 20 minutes, with durations of 1 to 4 hours for fentanyl and 2 to 6 hours for sufentanil. Despite intrathecal delivery, much of their analgesic effect arises from systemic absorption and central action in the brainstem, where sedation and respiratory depression may occur. Morphine, by contrast, remains concentrated at the dorsal horn of the spinal cord due to slower uptake, providing more sustained spinal-level analgesia.</p>
        <p>Adverse effects of lipophilic opioids are usually mild and short-lived, most commonly pruritus, with few other clinical concerns. Hydrophilic opioids share the adverse effect profile seen with systemic administration, making adverse effects, eg, delayed respiratory depression, more significant.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Perioperative Indications for&#x000a0;Spinal Opioids</bold>
</p>
        <p>When combined with a local anesthetic, spinal opioids improve the block's quality and reduce the need for systemic opioids postoperatively. For procedures that begin rapidly after a spinal injection, eg, cesarean section, fentanyl (10 to 20 &#x000b5;g) is often added due to its rapid onset to improve analgesia during surgery, while intrathecal morphine is added to provide postoperative analgesia. During the cesarean section, the addition of spinal opioids to the local anesthetic helps block discomfort due to the manipulation of the uterus. For surgeries that require large incisions or median sternotomy, fentanyl may or may not be added, and intrathecal morphine is added to provide postoperative analgesia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic Pain Management With&#x000a0;Spinal Opioids</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intrathecal opioids have a place in treating cancer pain refractory to systemic opioids, nonopioid analgesics, and other nonpharmacologic pain treatments. Intrathecal administration is preferred over the epidural route as drug doses required via epidural are approximately 10 times higher and increase systemic adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Implantable intrathecal infusion systems are available that deliver drugs to the CNS. The intrathecal catheter is typically placed below L1 under fluoroscopy. The decision to implant a pump is made based on priorities of patient care and life expectancy. In patients with severe chronic pain, comfort should be the priority. If life expectancy is weeks to months, a catheter may be tunneled under the skin and connected to a subcutaneous port. If life expectancy is longer, fully implanted catheters and infusion pumps are selected. Both fixed-rate and programmable pumps that provide as-needed bolus doses are available. If an intrathecal infusion pump is considered, a trial is often performed first with a single-shot technique.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>The decision to administer spinal opioids for chronic pain management should be made after confirmation that more conventional treatments are ineffective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Furthermore, as with all chronic pain management, the clinician must perform a thorough assessment of the source of pain and its impact on daily living activities. Imaging studies may aid the decision regarding the pain management technique to be selected and are vital to rule out sources of pain that should be treated surgically, eg, a tumor compressing a nerve.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Spinal Opioid Benefits</bold>
</p>
        <p>Neuraxial blockade applied for surgery has been shown to reduce postoperative mortality and additional serious complications after surgery. These include deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, and respiratory depression, as well as transfusion requirements.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Inadequate analgesia during the postoperative period can also lead to numerous adverse consequences, including hemodynamic instability (tachycardia, hypertension, vasoconstriction), increased catabolism, impaired immune response, and altered platelet activation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;These are particularly undesirable in patients with numerous preexisting comorbidities or following cardiac surgery.</p>
        <p>The addition of opioids to local anesthetics intensifies the quality of the sensory block produced by local anesthetics alone. When appropriately used, intrathecal opioids provide analgesia that is often superior to that achieved via the epidural and intravenous (IV) routes. Furthermore, when used in appropriate (low) doses, intrathecal opioids result in an adverse effect profile similar to that of systemic opioids, with an exception: pruritus tends to occur more commonly with intrathecal opioids.&#x000a0;Finally, a spinal is easier to perform than an epidural.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Absolute&#x000a0;Contraindications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Absolute contraindications to spinal include</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patient refusal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Congenital or acquired bleeding disorder</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ingestion of anticoagulants</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Antiplatelet or thrombolytic agents</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infection at the needle insertion site</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Spinal is also contraindicated in patients with increased intracranial pressure, eg, due to an intracranial mass, as this has the potential to result in brainstem herniation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Before performing spinal anesthesia, clinicians should obtain a thorough history to screen for the presence of contraindications, including questions about mucosal bleeding, unusual bleeding after trauma, dental procedures, or surgery, as well as significant nosebleeds or heavy menstrual bleeding. The presence of petechiae, ecchymoses, or a history of abnormal bleeding warrants hematology consultation before proceeding. In the absence of such a history, spinal anesthesia can generally be performed if prothrombin time (PT), activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), and platelet count are normal, as these tests exclude most clinically relevant bleeding disorders. Nonetheless, mild forms of von Willebrand disease may remain undetected.</p>
        <p>However, no universally accepted platelet threshold exists below which spinal anesthesia should never be performed, and practice varies among clinicians, institutions, and patient populations. Older literature recommended a platelet count of at least 100&#x000d7;10<sup>9</sup>/L, but many practitioners now consider lower counts acceptable depending on the indication and overall clinical context. For instance, in patients with cancer experiencing severe pain or in obstetric patients where general anesthesia poses high risks due to morbid obesity or abnormal airway anatomy, the benefits of spinal anesthesia may outweigh the risks. In parturients without preeclampsia, some propose a safe threshold of 75&#x000d7;10<sup>9</sup>/L, while reports from Douglas et al (2001) and Tanaka et al describe successful spinal procedures with platelet counts below 75&#x000d7;10<sup>9</sup>/L in patients with idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) or gestational thrombocytopenia. These studies suggest that in nonpreeclamptic parturients without a history or signs of bleeding, counts as low as 50&#x000d7;10<sup>9</sup>/L may be acceptable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Choi et al reviewed 507 neuraxial techniques in patients at risk for bleeding, including individuals with ITP and platelet counts less than 50&#x000d7;10<sup>9</sup>/L.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r16">[16]</xref> In that series, patients with hemophilia or von Willebrand disease and reduced factor levels received supplementation before spinal anesthesia. A case of permanent paraplegia occurred in a patient whose undiagnosed hemophilia was not treated before the procedure, underscoring the importance of identifying underlying coagulopathies.</p>
        <p>Assessment of platelet function using viscoelastic testing may further guide decision-making in patients with thrombocytopenia. Abnormal results on thromboelastogram (TEG), rotational thromboelastogram, or Quantra typically indicate that spinal anesthesia should be avoided.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r17">[17]</xref> In severe preeclampsia with platelet counts under 100&#x000d7;10<sup>9</sup>/L, impaired platelet function can be detected by TEG parameters such as r-time, maximal amplitude, and alpha angle. The platelet function analyzer (PFA-100) may offer even greater sensitivity for identifying platelet dysfunction in this population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>International surveys of practice highlight ongoing variability and emphasize the importance of risk&#x02013;benefit discussions, including the potential for subependymal hemorrhage in high-risk patients, eg, those undergoing cardiac surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Relative&#x000a0;Contraindications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Relative contraindications to spinal anesthesia should be evaluated on an individual basis, with risks weighed against potential benefits. Conditions such as spinal stenosis, prior spinal surgery, and spina bifida present challenges because altered anatomy can complicate needle placement and produce unpredictable spread of local anesthetic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r20">[20]</xref> Reports have described an association between neuraxial techniques and nerve injury in patients with spinal stenosis, lumbar disk disease, or a history of spine surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r20">[20]</xref> However, this link cannot be considered causal, since the data come from a retrospective study without a control group of surgical patients who did not undergo neuraxial techniques, and confounding factors such as disease progression were not accounted for. Patients with multiple sclerosis may demonstrate variable responses to spinal anesthesia, sometimes experiencing prolonged motor or sensory blockade, yet evidence does not suggest that spinal anesthesia worsens disease symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Bacteremia is also considered by some to represent a relative contraindication, primarily due to concerns that a needle traversing a blood vessel might introduce bacteria into the epidural or subarachnoid space. Nonetheless, clinicians frequently perform diagnostic lumbar punctures in patients with a fever of unknown origin. Available evidence indicates that lumbar puncture does not increase the risk of meningitis compared with the incidence of spontaneous meningitis. Cases labeled as lumbar puncture&#x02013;induced meningitis may actually represent meningitis from subsequent hematogenous seeding in patients with significant bacteremia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r22">[22]</xref> These findings support the view that spinal anesthesia can be performed when judged safer than general anesthesia, particularly if the patient has already demonstrated a clinical response to antibiotic therapy initiated before spinal puncture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r23">[23]</xref> In children younger than 1 year, however, studies have reported a higher incidence of meningitis following lumbar puncture during bacteremia, underscoring the need for heightened caution in this population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemodynamic Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hypovolemia, hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, and mitral stenosis represent relative contraindications to spinal anesthesia with local anesthetics. In these conditions, spinal-induced reductions in systemic vascular resistance can precipitate profound hypotension in patients who depend heavily on preload. Severe aortic stenosis requires even greater caution, as spinal injection of local anesthetic should be regarded as contraindicated. Patients with severe aortic stenosis have hypertrophied left ventricles with elevated oxygen demands, and reductions in diastolic blood pressure and coronary perfusion pressure may trigger myocardial ischemia. In select cases of mild or moderate valvular heart disease, low-dose intrathecal opioid administration without local anesthetic may be tolerated, as this produces smaller reductions in blood pressure. However, spinal techniques should be avoided in severe aortic stenosis, because once ventricular fibrillation occurs in this population, resuscitation is extremely difficult or impossible.</p>
        <p>Successful resuscitation in severe aortic stenosis often requires open-chest cardiac massage, as conventional efforts may fail. Some clinicians have reported the use of spinal anesthesia in patients with aortic stenosis, but these cases involved individuals with aortic valve areas of approximately 0.8 cm&#x000b2;, indicating noncritical stenosis, and included concurrent phenylephrine infusions to maintain blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r25">[25]</xref> Such reports highlight the need for careful patient selection and aggressive hemodynamic support. A thorough risk&#x02013;benefit assessment remains essential when considering spinal anesthesia with local anesthetics in patients with moderate aortic stenosis, while in cases of severe aortic stenosis, the technique should be regarded as absolutely contraindicated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Equipment required for spinal injection of opioids is the same as for spinal anesthesia performed with local anesthetic alone, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile gloves, a mask, and a sterile hat</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Antiseptic solution</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile overlay of the procedure field</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitors, including a pulse oximeter, an electrocardiogram (ECG), and a blood pressure cuff</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spinal anesthesia kit containing spinal needles, a local anesthetic for subcutaneous injection at the site, and a local anesthetic for spinal injection (some kits may contain labels and a sterile surgical marker)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Resuscitation equipment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If the patient is pregnant, fetal monitoring may be advisable depending on gestational age</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Neuraxial opioid procedures are typically performed by an anesthesiologist, a clinician who has completed a fellowship in pain management, or a spine specialist (either orthopedist or neurosurgeon). A trained assistant is advisable to aid the performing clinician, such as handing items in a sterile fashion as needed.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>History and coagulation tests should be used to exclude the possibility of a bleeding disorder. Questions related to drug ingestion should be asked as well, as medications may increase bleeding risk. The clinician should inquire about large-volume alcohol ingestion, as alcohol potentiates aspirin-induced and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced prolonged bleeding time for up to 2 days.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Spinal can usually be performed safely if patients take 325 mg of aspirin daily or a low dose of a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug. Notably, the number of days that drugs should be discontinued before regional anesthesia varies for different anticoagulants, antiplatelet agents, and herbal agents, based on their pharmacology. Therefore, the practitioner should refer to the 2018 guidelines of the American Society of Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine to ensure a safe amount of time has passed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Day of Procedure</bold>
</p>
        <p>Preparatory steps for spinal anesthesia remain consistent whether opioids are added to local anesthetic for surgery or administered alone in the pain management setting for chronic pain. Informed consent must always be obtained and should include a clear explanation of the risks and benefits of the spinal procedure. Patients should be advised about both mild adverse effects, such as nausea and pruritus, and more serious complications, including headache and neurologic injury. Postdural puncture headache (PDPH) occurs in approximately 1.5% to 2% of young, pregnant women, though newer studies report incidences as high as 4%. Persistent PDPH has led to medicolegal claims, making it important to explain the risk thoroughly to parturients. Patients should also be informed that spinal anesthesia for cesarean section is safer than general anesthesia, and that effective treatments, including epidural blood patch, are available if PDPH develops.</p>
        <p>Establishing a strong clinician&#x02013;patient relationship during the consent process promotes trust and facilitates acceptance of treatment if complications occur. In contrast, insufficient consent often results in patient dissatisfaction, especially when pain is compounded by feelings of neglect. Neurologic deficits, while rare, should be addressed, with an estimated incidence of 1:10,000 to 1:25,000, and patients should be reassured that most resolve over time. These figures are largely based on the Third National Audit Project of the Royal College of Anaesthetists, which provides comprehensive data on complications of neuraxial blocks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Some clinicians consider subependymal hemorrhage (SEH) too rare to warrant discussion, yet a more prudent approach includes acknowledging the risk while emphasizing its rarity, estimated at 1 in 100,000 to 1 in 200,000 in patients without coagulopathy. In certain institutions, consent for anesthesia is incorporated into the general surgical consent. In these cases, documentation of a specific discussion regarding spinal anesthesia remains essential.</p>
        <p>The healthcare team must also prepare for unforeseen events. Intravenous access should be established, and continuous monitoring with ECG, pulse oximetry, and noninvasive blood pressure should be maintained throughout the procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r30">[30]</xref> Equipment for advanced airway management must be immediately available, and staff should be trained in its use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r30">[30]</xref> Before needle placement, a procedure timeout and checklist verification help ensure patient safety and team readiness.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>When spinal anesthesia is used to supplement general anesthesia for major surgery, the placement usually occurs while the patient remains awake. This allows the patient to report pain or paresthesia, which may alert the anesthesiologist that the needle has contacted a nerve root. A slight withdrawal of the needle may then prevent intraneural injection, a factor some believe reduces the likelihood of postoperative neurologic complications, although the evidence remains inconclusive. In children, spinal anesthesia is frequently performed under general anesthesia, and no convincing evidence suggests this practice increases neurologic risk.</p>
        <p>The choice between sitting and lateral decubitus positioning lacks clear superiority, though the midline landmark is typically easier to identify in the sitting posture. In this position, patients sit with their back perpendicular to the table, resting their elbows on a support or hugging a pillow, while flexing their spine to open the interspaces. An assistant often helps maintain the posture and encourages maximal lumbar flexion. In the lateral decubitus position, patients adopt a fetal posture with knees drawn toward the chest and neck flexed, which also enhances interspace exposure. Anatomical landmarks guide introducer placement, with an imaginary line drawn across the iliac crests most often aligning with the L4 spinous process or the L4-L5 interspace. However, a puncture may occasionally occur at adjacent levels. Spinous processes generally mark the midline, while the intergluteal cleft may assist in obese patients where palpation proves difficult.</p>
        <p>Whether a single-shot spinal or continuous catheter is chosen, two primary insertion techniques&#x02014;midline and paramedian&#x02014;are available. After skin preparation with an antiseptic solution and application of a sterile fenestrated drape, local infiltration, usually with lidocaine 1% to 2%, anesthetizes the insertion site. The introducer is advanced at a 10 to 15 degrees cephalad angle, followed by passage of the spinal needle (commonly 24&#x02013;27 gauge) with its bevel parallel to the midline. Distinct tactile feedback occurs as the needle traverses the ligaments, with a characteristic &#x0201c;pop&#x0201d; felt on dural penetration. The average insertion depth in adults is about 5 cm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>If the midline approach fails due to arthritis, kyphoscoliosis, previous surgery, or calcified ligaments, a paramedian technique may be used. Here, the needle enters 1 cm caudal and 1 cm lateral to the tip of the L4 spinous process, angled 10 to 15 degrees cephalad and medially, thereby bypassing the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments. After dural puncture, clear CSF confirms correct placement before medication injection. Occasionally, blood-stained CSF appears initially but clears with continued flow, and injection may proceed in such cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>In patients undergoing cardiac surgery, the estimated risk of SEH after an atraumatic tap ranges from 1 in 220,000 to 1 in 3,600.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r33">[33]</xref> A traumatic tap increases the risk, prompting consideration of delaying surgery for 24 hours, especially when anticoagulation with agents (eg, heparin) is planned.&#x000a0;Spinal opioids administered for postoperative pain require a decision on timing. Administration before surgery reduces the surgical stress response and intraoperative anesthetic requirements, while providing preemptive analgesia that may confer benefits lasting up to 1 year.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r34">[34]</xref> Although injection at the end of surgery prolongs postoperative analgesia, the advantages of preemptive dosing generally outweigh this benefit.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Adverse Effects</bold>
</p>
        <p>Backache may develop after a spinal anesthetic procedure due to bruising or localized inflammation. Management typically involves cold compresses and oral analgesics (eg, acetaminophen or NSAIDs), with symptoms usually resolving within several days. Muscle spasm can be treated with muscle relaxants. Persistent or severe back pain warrants evaluation for more serious complications.</p>
        <p>Shivering occurs in at least 40% of patients receiving a spinal block with local anesthetic and is thought to result from vasodilation and a decrease in core temperature. Although opioids do not cause shivering, measures, eg, prewarming the patient and administering warm IV fluids, can reduce discomfort. Effective pharmacologic treatments include IV meperidine, tramadol, and clonidine.</p>
        <p>Adverse effects of spinal opioids, including nausea, vomiting, urinary retention, pruritus, and respiratory depression, mirror those seen with intravenous administration, though pruritus is often more pronounced with intrathecal delivery. Historical reports of severe respiratory depression and arrest involved excessive intrathecal opioid doses. Most adverse effects are dose-dependent; intrathecal morphine doses above 0.5 mg carry a higher risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r35">[35]</xref> Pruritus is particularly pronounced with sufentanil compared with fentanyl, contributing to fentanyl&#x02019;s more frequent use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r36">[36]</xref> Small doses of naloxone, 40 to 80 &#x000b5;g, effectively reduce pruritus without reversing analgesia, and ondansetron also diminishes severity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r37">[37]</xref> Neuraxial opioids may induce urinary retention via spinal and pontine micturition centers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Respiratory depression presents a serious concern due to its potentially life-threatening yet often insidious nature. No universal definition exists; however, clinical surrogates include decreased respiratory rate, oxygen saturation below 92%, the need for supplemental oxygen or airway intervention, and sedation requiring more than verbal stimulation. Patients may appear comfortable while hypercarbia contributes to subtle hypoventilation. Respiratory depression from intrathecal morphine typically occurs 6 to 18 hours after injection, though earlier onset can occur with concomitant IV narcotics. Low intraoperative and postoperative intravenous opioid dosing is advised when intrathecal morphine is administered. Evidence supports effective analgesia with intrathecal morphine doses as low as 0.15 to 0.3 mg for pelvic or abdominal surgery and 0.3 mg for median sternotomy. Postoperative patient-controlled analgesia with low-dose IV fentanyl or morphine optimizes pain control while minimizing risk. For same-day surgical procedures, intrathecal fentanyl should be limited to 5 to 10 &#x000b5;g. Continuous monitoring of patients receiving intrathecal morphine remains essential to detect and manage potential respiratory depression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Severe Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Failure of a spinal blockade does not constitute a complication in itself, but it requires careful assessment and management. Causes of block failure include needle movement during injection, incomplete entry of the needle into the subarachnoid space, or inadvertent injection into a nerve root. If a spinal with local anesthetic fails, proceeding with general anesthesia is advisable. Attempting a second dose may result in a high spinal, which carries risks of severe hypotension, bradycardia, respiratory arrest, and cardiac arrest.</p>
        <p>Postdural puncture headache (PDPH) represents a moderate complication influenced by needle size and patient demographics. Women, pregnant patients, younger individuals, and those with a history of headaches are at increased risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r39">[39]</xref> In a study of 94 cesarean patients using a 24-gauge Quincke needle, the sitting position was associated with a higher incidence of PDPH (20.8%) compared with the lateral decubitus position (4.3%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x000a0;= 0.017).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;Another study of 320 obstetric patients found a PDPH incidence of 5% with a 25-gauge Whitacre needle versus 28.12% with a 25-gauge Quincke needle, potentially reflecting the duration of postoperative follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;The high incidence of reported PDPH in the Quincke group may be because patients were asked about symptoms of headache for 5 days postop.</p>
        <p>Major complications remain rare when proper technique is followed, but they include direct needle trauma, infection, peripheral nerve injury, and SEH. Needle insertion into the spinal cord or nerves can cause paresthesia; a retrospective study found 6.3% of patients experienced paresthesia during insertion, yet only 0.67% had persistent symptoms, all resolving within 24 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Respiratory depression from spinal opioids is dose-dependent and influenced by concurrent IV narcotics. Local anesthetics for surgical anesthesia may also result in high spinal with bradycardia, hypotension, respiratory arrest, or cardiac arrest. These events require prompt treatment with atropine, ephedrine, phenylephrine, or epinephrine, along with intubation or advanced cardiac life support. Rapid epinephrine administration is critical in preventing brain injury following cardiac arrest after spinal.</p>
        <p>Meningitis may occur after spinal anesthesia, with potential sources including contaminated trays or medications, oral flora of the performing clinician, or preexisting patient infection. The incidence following diagnostic lumbar puncture does not differ significantly from that of spontaneous cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Spinal epidural hematoma represents the most severe complication. New neurologic symptoms after spinal anesthesia warrant immediate neurosurgical consultation and spinal magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), as outcomes improve with surgery within 24 hours of symptom onset.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r44">[44]</xref> A recent study assessing risk factors for SEH after spinal surgery, even when a spinal was not performed, identified systolic blood pressure increases of 50 mm Hg or more after extubation and high body mass index as significant contributors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r45">[45]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Intrathecal opioids, whether combined with local anesthetics for surgical anesthesia or administered alone, blunt the intraoperative stress response, reduce the requirement for anesthetic agents, and enhance postoperative analgesia. Low-dose fentanyl, 10 to 20 &#x000b5;g, is commonly added to local anesthetics for short or minimally invasive procedures, whereas intrathecal morphine is typically used for surgeries involving larger incisions. Administration of intrathecal opioids is straightforward, requiring no specialized equipment or advanced technical skills. Their specific applications vary depending on the surgical type and patient needs.&#x000a0;Intrathecal opioids also provide effective management for patients with severe chronic pain syndromes who do not achieve adequate relief through conventional treatments.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Foot and Ankle Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>For foot and ankle surgery, which is often performed on an outpatient basis, fentanyl in doses of 5 to 10 &#x000b5;g is generally preferred. This approach can improve the quality of the spinal block while extending postoperative analgesia for up to 4 hours. However, some clinicians may avoid intrathecal opioids in this setting, as postoperative nausea, vomiting, or pruritus could delay timely discharge.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cesarean Section</bold>
</p>
        <p>Neuraxial anaesthesia is the preferred technique for caesarean deliveries in the United States and Canada, used for more than 95% of these procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;A combination of local anaesthetic (most often bupivacaine) and opioid (fentanyl and morphine) is frequently used. Other opioids, eg, diamorphine, mepredine, sufentanyl, have been described for intrathecal use during caesarean section. A meta-analysis of patients undergoing spinal anaesthesia for cesarean section found the need for intraoperative analgesic supplementation decreased from 24% to 4% when opioids were added to the local anaesthetic injection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;The preference for spinal (or epidural) anaesthesia over general anaesthesia is that regional techniques preclude the need to manage the airway, which is more challenging in the pregnant population. Furthermore, maternal blood loss is decreased with spinal compared to general anesthesia, primarily due to lower blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r47">[47]</xref> Spinal anaesthesia also allows the mother to be awake, have a partner present, and permits rapid bonding with the baby. Fentanyl 10 to 15 &#x000b5;g&#x000a0;is often administered to supplement intraoperative analgesia provided by the local anaesthetic, while it is added to 0.100 to 0.150 mg intrathecal morphine to provide postoperative analgesia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Orthopedic Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Spinal anaesthesia is beneficial for hip fracture surgery, particularly in the elderly, and a 2005 best practice review recommended regional anaesthesia whenever possible for this type of surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;Benefits cited include decreased mortality, deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary complications, and transfusion requirements. Intrathecal morphine has also been used as part of multimodal anaesthesia after total knee arthroplasty, with patients having lower pain scores at 12 hours postoperatively and lower systemic opioid use up to 48 hours postoperatively, compared with controls.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r49">[49]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cardiac Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Multiple groups have reported the use of intrathecal morphine in patients undergoing cardiac surgery. Several groups have reported decreased postoperative pain scores with intrathecal morphine and diminished postoperative systemic opioids compared to controls who received no intrathecal injection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Low-dose intrathecal morphine has also been shown to facilitate early extubation after cardiac surgery&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r53">[53]</xref>, including off-pump coronary artery bypass grafting, thereby facilitating fast-track recovery. In a study of 507 balanced pairs created with propensity score matching, patients who received intrathecal morphine had fewer postoperative pulmonary complications after heart surgery than patients treated with IV analgesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Additionally, fewer patients in the intrathecal morphine group required prolonged ICU or hospital stays.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;Another group reported improved pain scores, improved patient satisfaction, and decreased catecholamine release at some time points in patients treated with intrathecal morphine versus controls.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;In children ages 3 months to 6 years old undergoing pediatric cardiac surgery, spinal blockade with tetracaine 0.5&#x000a0;to 2.0 mg/kg and morphine 7 &#x000b5;g/kg has been shown to result in lower postoperative pain scores and lower IV fentanyl PCA requirements versus controls, with no differences in hypotension, bradycardia, oxygen desaturation, or pruritus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r55">[55]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Video-Assisted Thoracoscopic Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery represents a minimally invasive approach to thoracic procedures, offering advantages such as fewer pulmonary complications and faster recovery. Despite these benefits, patients often experience significant postoperative pain. Thoracic epidurals and fascial plane blocks are commonly used for analgesia, though both techniques present technical challenges. In contrast, single-shot intrathecal morphine provides a simpler and highly effective method of pain control in these patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Abdominal Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intrathecal morphine has been widely studied in intra-abdominal procedures, eg, pancreaticoduodenectomy, nephrectomy, and surgeries for gynecological malignancies. Comparisons with epidural analgesia consistently demonstrate effective pain control, with intrathecal morphine offering the additional advantages of technical ease and improved hemodynamic stability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r58">[58]</xref>&#x000a0;Liver resection surgeries have also been performed successfully with the use of intrathecal morphine, showing similarly favorable outcomes in terms of postoperative analgesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r58">[58]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Monitoring After Administration of Spinal Opioids</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients who receive intrathecal morphine require close monitoring of respiratory rate and level of alertness. Standard practice involves hourly assessments for the first 12 hours, followed by evaluations every 2 hours from hours 12 to 24. Patients with comorbidities such as hypertension, cardiac or neurologic disease, body mass index of 40 or greater, use of sedatives, exposure to general anesthesia, or IV magnesium should undergo hourly monitoring for 24 hours. High-risk patients may benefit from the addition of capnometry or pulse oximetry, though clinicians must recognize that desaturation often develops late in patients receiving supplemental oxygen. Slowing respiratory rate and increasing sedation frequently signal respiratory depression earlier. A respiratory rate of 10 breaths per minute warrants immediate bedside evaluation, and order sets should mandate clinician notification at or below this threshold. Obese patients, particularly those with obstructive sleep apnea or concomitant sedative use, face a higher risk of respiratory depression and should be monitored for extended periods.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r60">[60]</xref></p>
        <p>For obstetric patients, the American Society of Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology published less restrictive guidelines in 2019 to avoid unnecessary withholding of intrathecal morphine in settings where monitoring may not be available. These guidelines apply primarily to cesarean patients, who are typically young, healthy, and receive a single intrathecal morphine dose without additional sedation. No extra monitoring is recommended when the dose is less than 50 &#x000b5;g. For doses between 50 &#x000b5;g and 150 &#x000b5;g, respiratory rate and sedation should be checked every 2 hours for 12 hours. For doses above 150 &#x000b5;g, the standard monitoring outlined above applies.</p>
        <p>The 2021 NICE guidelines specifically address the use of intrathecal diamorphine for cesarean delivery. Routine monitoring is not advised except for patients at risk of respiratory depression, eg, those with obesity or obstructive sleep apnea. Even in these patients, 12 hours of hourly monitoring is considered sufficient.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traditional teaching places the termination of the adult spinal cord at L1, yet ultrasound imaging has demonstrated the conus medullaris extending below L1 in some individuals. Furthermore, palpation of the iliac crest, often assumed to represent the L4-L5 interspace, may occasionally correspond to a more cephalad level, potentially explaining episodes of paresthesia during spinal anesthesia.&#x000a0;Symptoms, eg, nausea, dizziness, or a sensation of feeling strange shortly after spinal anesthesia, often reflect hypotension not yet measured by the monitor. Administering a small dose of phenylephrine or ephedrine&#x02014;selected based on heart rate&#x02014;while the blood pressure cuff cycles can correct hypotension if present and poses little risk if not.</p>
        <p>During the postoperative night, reliance on pulse oximetry alone may provide false reassurance, as patients on supplemental oxygen can maintain saturations despite a dangerously slow respiratory rate. Orders should mandate clinician notification if the respiratory rate falls below 10 breaths/min. Naloxone should be administered at 40 &#x000b5;g IV for a respiratory rate of 8 breaths/min, with an additional 80 &#x000b5;g IV if the rate does not increase to at least 10 breaths/min within 2 minutes. Patients with decreased arousal should undergo arterial blood gas testing; if carbon dioxide pressure is elevated, low-dose naloxone infusion or ventilatory support (eg, BiPAP or controlled ventilation) may be necessary.</p>
        <p>Some clinicians advocate replacing the stylet once clear CSF appears and advancing the needle an additional 1 mm. This approach ensures the entire bevel lies within the spinal canal, reducing the risk of incomplete drug delivery and block failure. In certain populations, such as elderly patients with narrowed interspaces and a reduced risk of post-dural puncture headache, some practitioners prepare a larger needle, eg, 22-gauge, on the sterile tray.</p>
        <p>Although spinal procedures are often assumed to be the cause of spinal epidural hematoma (SEH occurring shortly after intervention, spontaneous SEH can also arise independently. This consideration underscores the importance of carefully evaluating any new neurological symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29352.r63">[63]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29352.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Spinal anesthesia, often combined with intrathecal opioids, provides effective surgical anesthesia and prolonged postoperative pain relief with a long-established record of safety. By supplementing local anesthetics, spinal opioids enhance analgesia without producing sympathetic or motor blockade, making them valuable in both perioperative and chronic pain management. Their use requires careful consideration of pharmacologic properties, patient factors, and potential adverse effects to ensure safe and effective outcomes.</p>
        <p>Optimal patient-centered care relies on coordinated skills and responsibilities across the interprofessional team. Physicians and advanced practitioners must assess indications, contraindications, and dosing strategies, while nurses provide vigilant monitoring for complications such as respiratory depression or pruritus. Pharmacists contribute expertise in drug selection, interactions, and patient education. General practitioners support continuity of care, especially in the management of chronic pain, while other allied health professionals reinforce patient engagement and promote functional recovery. Effective communication and shared decision-making among team members ensure safe analgesic delivery, reduce risks, and enhance outcomes. This collaborative strategy strengthens patient safety, optimizes postoperative recovery, and supports long-term pain control.</p>
      </sec>
